module

public import Mathlib.Probability.Kernel.CondDistrib

/-!
# Regular conditional distributions

A regular conditional distribution is a version of conditional probability
that is, almost surely, a genuine probability measure. There are two
textbook packagings: a *family* of measures indexed by the sample point, and a *Markov kernel* indexed by the value of the
conditioning random variable.

## Source

This formalizes the target "Definition (Regular conditional distribution)"
(Lecture 26) and the target "Theorem (Existence of regular conditional
probabilities)" (Lecture 26) of `v1/Atlas/TheoryOfProbability/targets.yaml`,
following `v1/Atlas/TheoryOfProbability/code/RegularConditionalDistribution.lean`
and `v1/Atlas/TheoryOfProbability/code/ExistRegularCondProb.lean` in v1.

## Scope

In:

* `ProbabilityTheory.RegularConditionalDistribution`: the family form.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsRegularConditionalDistribution`: the kernel form.
* `ProbabilityTheory.condDistrib_isRegularConditionalDistribution`:
  Mathlib's `condDistrib` is a regular conditional distribution.
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_regularConditionalDistribution`: existence in
  the kernel form, for any conditioning random variable.
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_regularConditionalDistribution_family`:
  existence in the family form for the σ-algebra `σ(X)` generated by the
  conditioning random variable, with witness `ω ↦ condDistrib Y X μ (X ω)`.

Out: existence in the family form for a general sub-σ-algebra `𝒢`, which
v1 does not prove either (v1's existence theorem is the kernel form).
That general case needs a countable-generating-algebra construction and
is left for a follow-up.

## Relation to Mathlib

Mathlib has `ProbabilityTheory.condDistrib` and the defining lemma
`condDistrib_ae_eq_condExp`, but it has no packaged notion of a regular
conditional distribution in either form above, and no existence theorem
stating that `condDistrib` is one. The two structures here are that
packaging; the proofs are one-line applications of Mathlib's lemmas.

## Main results

* `ProbabilityTheory.RegularConditionalDistribution`
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsRegularConditionalDistribution`
* `ProbabilityTheory.condDistrib_isRegularConditionalDistribution`
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_regularConditionalDistribution`
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_regularConditionalDistribution_family`
-/

@[expose] public section

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace ProbabilityTheory

variable {α β Ω : Type*} {mα : MeasurableSpace α} {mβ : MeasurableSpace β}
  [MeasurableSpace Ω] {μ : Measure α}

/-- **Regular conditional distribution (textbook family form).** A family of
measures `rcd : Ω → Measure S` is a regular conditional distribution for `X`
given the sub-σ-algebra `𝒢` under `P` if:
(1) for every measurable `A ⊆ S`, the map `ω ↦ rcd ω A` is a version of
`P(X ∈ A | 𝒢)`, and
(2) for `P`-a.e. `ω`, the map `A ↦ rcd ω A` is a probability measure. -/
structure RegularConditionalDistribution
    {Ω : Type*} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S]
    (rcd : Ω → Measure S) (X : Ω → S) (𝒢 : MeasurableSpace Ω)
    (P : @Measure Ω mΩ) : Prop where
  ae_eq_condexp : ∀ {A : Set S}, MeasurableSet A →
    (fun ω => (rcd ω A).toReal) =ᵐ[P] P⟦X ⁻¹' A | 𝒢⟧
  ae_isProbabilityMeasure : ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, IsProbabilityMeasure (rcd ω)

/-- **Regular conditional distribution (kernel form).** A Markov kernel
`κ : Kernel β Ω` is a regular conditional distribution of `Y : α → Ω` given
`X : α → β` under `μ` if for every measurable `s ⊆ Ω`, the function
`a ↦ κ (X a) s` is a version of the conditional probability
`μ(Y ∈ s | σ(X))`. -/
structure IsRegularConditionalDistribution
    (κ : Kernel β Ω) (Y : α → Ω) (X : α → β) (μ : Measure α) : Prop where
  isMarkovKernel : IsMarkovKernel κ
  ae_eq_condExp : ∀ {s : Set Ω}, MeasurableSet s →
    (fun a => (κ (X a)).real s) =ᵐ[μ] μ⟦Y ⁻¹' s | mβ.comap X⟧

/-- When `Ω` is a standard Borel space, Mathlib's `condDistrib Y X μ` is a
regular conditional distribution of `Y` given `X` under `μ`. This packages
`condDistrib_ae_eq_condExp` together with the Markov-kernel property. -/
theorem condDistrib_isRegularConditionalDistribution
    [StandardBorelSpace Ω] [Nonempty Ω] [IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    {X : α → β} {Y : α → Ω}
    (hX : Measurable X) (hY : Measurable Y) :
    IsRegularConditionalDistribution (condDistrib Y X μ) Y X μ where
  isMarkovKernel := inferInstance
  ae_eq_condExp hs := condDistrib_ae_eq_condExp hX hY hs

/-- **Existence of regular conditional distributions (kernel form).** When
`Ω` is a standard Borel space, for any finite measure `μ` and measurable
maps `X : α → β`, `Y : α → Ω`, there exists a Markov kernel
`κ : Kernel β Ω` that is a regular conditional distribution of `Y` given
`X`. -/
theorem exists_regularConditionalDistribution
    [StandardBorelSpace Ω] [Nonempty Ω] [IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    {X : α → β} {Y : α → Ω}
    (hX : Measurable X) (hY : Measurable Y) :
    ∃ κ : Kernel β Ω, IsRegularConditionalDistribution κ Y X μ :=
  ⟨condDistrib Y X μ, condDistrib_isRegularConditionalDistribution hX hY⟩

/-- **Existence of regular conditional distributions (family form).** When
`Ω` is a standard Borel space, for any finite measure `μ` and measurable
maps `X : α → β`, `Y : α → Ω`, there exists a family `rcd : α → Measure Ω`
that is a regular conditional distribution of `Y` given the σ-algebra
`σ(X)` generated by `X`, namely `rcd ω = condDistrib Y X μ (X ω)`. -/
theorem exists_regularConditionalDistribution_family
    [StandardBorelSpace Ω] [Nonempty Ω] [IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    {X : α → β} {Y : α → Ω}
    (hX : Measurable X) (hY : Measurable Y) :
    ∃ rcd : α → Measure Ω,
      RegularConditionalDistribution rcd Y (mβ.comap X) μ := by
  refine ⟨fun a => condDistrib Y X μ (X a), ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro A hA
    exact condDistrib_ae_eq_condExp hX hY hA
  · exact Filter.Eventually.of_forall fun _ => inferInstance

end ProbabilityTheory
